windows: daemon over a protected named pipe with a session key, protected processes, no loopback servers

Clauseo's patch to vercel-labs/agent-browser v0.41.1, applied after 0001. Windows code paths
only; macOS and Linux behave as upstream (plus 0001).

- The daemon listens on \\.\pipe\clauseo-ab-<128-bit random hex> instead of loopback TCP, with
  four instances listening at once. <session>.pipe records the pipe id with the daemon's pid and
  creation time and their HMAC-SHA256 with the session key (never a command-line argument); a
  record without a valid HMAC is ignored. The first instance is created with
  FILE_FLAG_FIRST_PIPE_INSTANCE; every instance has a protected DACL for the user and SYSTEM and
  rejects remote clients. After a client connects the daemon reads its process token
  (GetNamedPipeClientProcessId, OpenProcessToken, IsTokenRestricted) and disconnects a restricted
  client, or any it cannot check, before writing a byte.
- The session key (session_key.rs): the launcher passes 32 random bytes per session in
  AGENT_BROWSER_SESSION_KEY (64 lowercase hex). Every connection starts with a mutual
  challenge-response: each side sends a fresh 32-byte nonce and proves the key with HMAC-SHA256
  over its role, the session name and both nonces, compared in constant time. The daemon serves
  no client without the key; the client refuses a daemon of another session or key. main takes
  the key out of the process environment first thing; spawn_hardened gives it back to its
  children only (the daemon, the MCP server's CLI runs). Without a valid key the CLI and the
  daemon refuse to run. hmac and sha2 are already upstream dependencies.
- The client connects at SECURITY_IDENTIFICATION and refuses a pipe whose server is not the
  recorded daemon: the same pid and creation time (so a reused pid fails), this executable, and an
  unrestricted token.
- A live daemon (a keyed record, pid and creation time match) keeps its files and is not
  replaced: a busy pipe is waited for, and the CLI does not start a second daemon over it. The
  taskkill fallback is gone (the protected DACL denies PROCESS_TERMINATE anyway).
- Every agent-browser process applies
  D:P(A;;0x1FFFFF;;;SY)(A;;0x101000;;;<user SID>)(A;;0x101000;;;OW) to itself and
  D:P(A;;0x1FFFFF;;;SY)(A;;0x100800;;;<user SID>)(A;;0x100800;;;OW) to its main thread first
  thing in main; the daemon's runtime threads get the thread DACL as they start; the daemon (from
  the CLI) and the CLI (from the MCP server) start with both as their process and first-thread
  descriptors through CreateProcessW, inheriting only their stdio handles.
- Streaming, `inspect` and the dashboard are always off on Windows (0001's switch forced on).
- Tests that start a daemon on Windows (tls_cli, obscura_cli) pass a test session key.
  Upstream's stream server tests (native::stream, and actions' stream enable tests) do not run
  on Windows; a Windows test checks that `stream enable` is refused there. The Windows probes
  run under a token as a sandbox gives one (windows_test_token.rs): a write-restricted token
  with the logon SID among its restricting SIDs (so the probe can open its desktop), or a
  filtered one without SeDebugPrivilege or Administrators; a failed check prints both tokens.
- tests/mcp_call_cli.rs (every OS, test only): the subprocess timeout (10 s) can be raised with
  AB_TEST_SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT_SECS, which the build workflow sets for x86_64 macOS under Rosetta;
  the user-config test does not run on Windows, where dirs::home_dir() ignores HOME and
  USERPROFILE.

